It's not often one says this in relation to The Walking Dead, but maybe things are going to turn out OK, after all?
Two plodding instalments on the trot had even the most ardent fans fearing that the series was going nowhere fast. But with 'Sing Me a Song', the most inconsistent show on television delivers an outing that's mostly satisfying.
Helping to restore the faith – and compensating for his deathly dull canoodling with Enid two weeks back – is Carl Grimes, opening the episode with a dramatic arrival into the Sanctuary and a flurry of gunfire.
© AMC
Advertisement - Continue Reading Below
Of course, Carl's quickly overwhelmed by Negan's forces, but you've got to admire his bad-assery. Negan certainly does – rather than punish the teen, he takes him under his wing and offers a guided tour of his headquarters.
Negan's an engaging figure – for the audience and for Carl, offering a glimpse at world where he has absolute power, plus women and alcohol. Seductive stuff for a teenage boy.
But he's also a bully, and being part of a bully's gang is only fun till he turns his attentions onto you. So it is that when Negan gets bored, he forces Carl to remove his bandage and expose the gaping hole where his eye used to be.
It's a sequence taken pretty much word-for-word from the comics, though something is lost in translation. Carl's been bolshy and brave until now – refusing to look away when Negan murdered his friends – so it feels odd to have him be meek here, shaking and scared. It doesn't chime with what we've seen before, nor with his defiant behaviour later in the episode.
