Likewise, why does Negan even consider Carl "a boy" and not a man, when it was established just last week that he and his forces were more than happy to slaughter any male in a rival camp above the age of 10?
The problem comes from Chandler Riggs now being far older than the Carl of the comics. This sequence just doesn't make as much sense when it's a 17-year-old sitting opposite Negan. You'll be inclined to forgive 'Sing Me a Song' its narrative inconsistencies, though, because Riggs and Jeffrey Dean Morgan do terrific work throughout.
It might not hold together as well as it did in the source material, but the dynamic between Negan and Carl is still fascinating. Negan was always Rick's mirror image – the leader of his own settlement, willing to do whatever it takes to survive.
He's only a worse person because he seems to enjoy all the carnage so damn much, while Rick at least has the decency to act all tortured about it. So it makes sense for Negan to act as a father figure of sorts to Carl, eager to impress his surrogate son.
'Sing Me a Song' is already up on the past two weeks by offering an engaging central story that delivers both character development and plot advancement: elsewhere, Daryl looks set to attempt another prison break, while Michonne's also rediscovered her inner bad-ass.
But happily, even the sub-plots featuring second-tier characters have something to offer. In particular, Father Gabriel's continued character rehabilitation is wonderful to witness. "What you're saying doesn't make you a sinner, but it does make you a tremendous shit," he tells Spencer, when the last surviving Monroe wishes for Rick to meet a grisly end.
