Even before Parkland Memorial Hospital has survived a crucial survey to retain its government funding, its top managers are hoping to reinstate incentive pay for those who run the county facility.
This time, it's being called "pay for performance."
At a meeting with hospital board members Wednesday, Parkland's interim chief executive officer Bob Smith laid out a plan that would offer performance pay for an unspecified group of hospital managers. It would be based in part on the "balanced score card" Smith has been developing for Parkland.
As he described it, about 75 percent of each manager's performance pay would be tied to achieving certain hospital-wide improvements, such as how well Parkland holds down its 30-day readmission rates for heart failure patients or how well it stabilizes the turnover of first-year nurses.
The remaining 25 percent of the payout would reflect how well each manager fulfilled three to five individual measures tied to his or her specific job.
Those individual goals would be agreed upon by the employees before the start of the next fiscal year on Oct. 1, Smith told the board. The payment amounts would depend on budget considerations and how much each manager was already earning, he added.
In the past, the more money a manager earned, the higher the incentive pay. Dr. Ron Anderson, the hospital's previous CEO, received the largest such payout for 2010, adding $279,298 to his $606,070 paycheck that year.
"In the first year, there might be some dissatisfaction," Smith acknowledged this week. "You set goals and find out nobody achieves them. What you want to do is hold the carrot out there so that really superior performance is rewarded."
While no board vote has been taken yet on the plan, several members sounded enthusiastic about it.
"This is where the industry is going," said Dr. Winfred Parnell, a board member who also works at Medical City Dallas Hospital. "It drives accountability down into the management structure."
However, the current hospital board pulled the plug on incentive payments when Parkland became mired in patient-safety problems in late 2011. The hospital has been operating under federal oversight for over a year as it overhauls many of its patient practices to improve overall quality.
The board's discussion this week did not mention Parkland's short, but contentious, history of offering such incentive pay to its top managers. While the hospital's top three executives had received such payments for years, the fallout came when the program expanded to include more Parkland managers for fiscal 2009 and 2010.
Both times, rank-and-file workers complained loud and long that the practice was inherently unfair to them.
The Dallas Morning News had obtained the details of those management payouts through Texas open-records requests. Both times, the hospital board had finalized the payments in closed sessions without releasing any specifics.
The newspaper published both lists, including each manager's name, salary and incentive payment for that year. It was the first time Parkland employees knew how much their bosses were paid and how much they were able to supplement those paychecks.
For fiscal 2009, 27 of Parkland's top managers split $1.7 million in incentive pay, boosting their base salaries from 19 percent to 31 percent. The actual amounts ranged from $34,727 to $143,325.
For fiscal 2010, the program was expanded to more than 160 Parkland managers, including most department heads, and the payout topped $4 million. However, some managers received as little as a 4 percent boost, while the top payout was 46 percent.
In that same period, other Parkland employees qualified for 2 to 4 percent merit pay increases for each year. The difference angered some employees, who posted public comments on internet message boards, challenging the idea that the executives alone deserved credit for reaching hospital-wide performance goals.
