Cavemen really knew how to eat. Out of necessity, of course. Twenty thousand years ago, stress didn't come in the form of parking tickets and it wasn't caused by having to sprint through O'Hare to make your connection. Stress was famine. And the human body conditioned itself to respond to famine. When you eat too many bags of sea-salt-and-vinegar chips at work, your body still thinks it's preparing for impending starvation.
We have this new diet book called You: On a Diet. We didn't even want the word diet on the cover. (Our publisher, however, did.) It's not a traditional diet book -- it's not about obsessing over the number of calories in a Caesar salad. It's about changing the way you eat and focusing on neglected parts of your body, the ones no one has told you to think about until now. And that's a very different goal.
Here is our humble prediction: Our philosophy, the one laid out over the next several pages, just might change the way people think about food, health, and dieting. That's our hope, anyway. It's about doing things -- easy things -- that will naturally bring you to your ideal, healthy weight. You won't even have to think about it. In fact, if a diet requires you to ruminate very much, it will fail you.
More than anything else, the biology of blubber is about evolution. When the caveman felt stress due to impending starvation, his body responded in two brilliant ways. First, it lowered its metabolism. Second, it increased the tendency to store those unburned sugars as fat, especially around the belly (so we could easily feed our organs). Early man burned less of what he ate and saved more; when famine hit, he was ready.
The technique worked even as late as when your grandfather was young, a time when the typical American man's life was an active one. If you weren't a farmer, you were likely a factory worker. You moved all day. Even if you worked in an office, you at least ate natural foods, because that's all there was. But in the past, say, half century, we've screwed up the relationship between everyday living and lifelong health. We've tried to make the world adapt to us. We still feel chronic stress -- the parking tickets, the missed flight -- but our body's response hasn't changed: Burn fewer calories, store more fat. The problem is, when our ancestors reached for an apple, we go for those peanut-butter-and-cracker sandwiches from the vending machine. And most of us sit at desks all day. When we work out, we do so hastily.
Advertisement - Continue Reading Below
It's time for your body to sync up with the environment again. Evolution takes too long to catch up with you -- the human body isn't going to figure out how to process Crisco in your lifetime -- so it's you who has to adapt. Easy. You're about to learn how the brain affects what and why you eat, how your guts affect your brain and body, and how your muscles are the key. All of which will help you reach your 18,000 B.C. weight. Say what you will about cavemen, but not one of them had a beer belly.
